## 3. A reliable key to join goalscorers, results and shootouts

```sql


-- 3a. Persist the key as a real, indexed column on each table, so joins
ALTER TABLE results     ADD COLUMN match_key TEXT;
ALTER TABLE goalscorers ADD COLUMN match_key TEXT;
ALTER TABLE shootouts   ADD COLUMN match_key TEXT;

UPDATE results     SET match_key = LOWER(TRIM(date)) || '|' || LOWER(TRIM(home_team)) || '|' || LOWER(TRIM(away_team));
UPDATE goalscorers SET match_key = LOWER(TRIM(date)) || '|' || LOWER(TRIM(home_team)) || '|' || LOWER(TRIM(away_team));
UPDATE shootouts   SET match_key = LOWER(TRIM(date)) || '|' || LOWER(TRIM(home_team)) || '|' || LOWER(TRIM(away_team));

CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ix_results_key     ON results(match_key);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ix_goalscorers_key ON goalscorers(match_key);
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ix_shootouts_key ON shootouts(match_key);

-- 3b. Uniqueness check on results (should return only the 2 known dupes)
SELECT match_key, COUNT(*) AS n
FROM results
GROUP BY match_key
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

-- 3c. Example: all three joined together on the key
SELECT r.match_key,
       r.date, r.home_team, r.away_team, r.home_score, r.away_score, r.tournament,
       s.winner        AS shootout_winner,
       g.team          AS scoring_team,
       g.scorer, g.minute, g.own_goal, g.penalty
FROM results r
LEFT JOIN shootouts   s ON s.match_key = r.match_key
LEFT JOIN goalscorers g ON g.match_key = r.match_key;

-- 3d. Orphan check: shootouts with no matching result
SELECT s.*
FROM shootouts s
LEFT JOIN results r ON r.match_key = s.match_key
WHERE r.match_key IS NULL;
```

**Why this key:**
- **Deterministic:** the same match gets the same key in every file and on every reload. An auto-increment ID depends on load order.
- **Normalised:** `TRIM` and `LOWER` stop whitespace or case differences from breaking joins.
- **Indexed:** I stored it as a column with indexes (unique on `shootouts`). The first version computed it in views and was too slow to join.

**Validation:**

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| goalscorers → results | 43,189 / 43,189 matched (100%) |
| shootouts → results | 561 / 562 matched |
| Duplicate keys in shootouts | 0 |
| Duplicate keys in results | 2 (see below) |

**Issues found:**
- `results` has 2 fixtures listed twice with different scores: 1974-02-17 Tahiti v New Caledonia (2-1 and 1-2), and 1977-10-22 Guyana v Barbados (2-0 in Linden, 0-0 in Georgetown). No goal or shootout rows reference them, so the joins aren't affected. In production I'd add a sequence number or deduplicate at source.
- 1 orphan shootout: 2011-06-29 Saare County v Åland Islands (Island Games). No result exists for that date. The closest match is 2011-06-26 Åland Islands 3-3 Saare County, which has the teams swapped and a different date.
- Team names change over time (`Åland` vs `Åland Islands`). A team-name mapping table would make the key more robust.
- SQLite's `LOWER` only lowercases ASCII letters. It still applies the same way in every table, so the key stays consistent.

